cleanx v1.7.0 — Fast, safe disk & inode cleaner (Bash)
Usage:
  cleanx [options] <tasks...>

Tasks:
  report packages apt apt-residuals journal logs tmp tmpfiles usercache langcaches snap flatpak
  docker podman containerd kernels coredumps buildcache browsers timeshift
  pkgbig inode-scan fshints all

General Options:
  --dry-run                 default; show actions only
  --yes                     apply changes (disables dry-run)
  --aggressive              stronger cleanup (e.g., Docker/Podman volumes)
  --low-impact              run with ionice/nice to reduce pressure
  --force                   suppress the >=98% rootfs warning
  --secure-erase            use shred on files before removal (slow)
  --quota=SIZE              stop after reclaiming SIZE (e.g., 2G, 800M)
  --json                    emit only JSON on stdout; operational output goes to stderr
  --config=PATH             extra declarative KEY=VALUE config file
  --channel=NAME            channel for --check-update (default: stable); --update stays stable

Tuning:
  --journal-keep=VAL        e.g., 7d or 200M (default: 7d)
  --log-max=SIZE            truncate *.log bigger than SIZE (default: 100M)
  --tmp-days=N              delete /tmp entries older than N days (default: 7)
  --snap-retain=N           keep N snap revisions (default: 2)
  --user=NAME               target user's caches (default: runner)
  --inode-path=PATH         path for inode-scan (default: .)
  --inode-depth=N           depth for inode-scan (default: 3)
  --inode-top=N             show top N for inode-scan (default: 50)
  --exclude-glob=GLOB       add a global exclusion (repeatable)
  --include-glob=GLOB       add a name include filter (repeatable)

Utility:
  --version                 print version and exit
  --check-update            compare local version with remote (uses --channel)
  --update                  self-update from the latest checksum-verified stable release
  --print-config            show effective configuration
  --doctor                  environment/requirements check
  --completions=bash|zsh    print shell completions to stdout
  --help                    show this help

Examples:
  cleanx --report
  sudo cleanx --yes --low-impact apt logs tmp tmpfiles usercache langcaches journal
  sudo cleanx --yes --aggressive docker podman containerd
  sudo cleanx --yes --secure-erase --quota=5G usercache tmp logs
  cleanx --report inode-scan --inode-path=/var --inode-depth=4 --inode-top=100
